    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>Tests for a good health metric</h2>
  <p>Before adding a metric to a health view, it is worth putting it through five questions. Metrics that fail more than one tend to become wallpaper.</p>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Does it sit upstream of an outcome we care about</strong>, through a mechanism we can explain? If not, it may be interesting, but it is not a health metric.</li>
    <li><strong>Do we know what normal looks like?</strong> If nobody can describe its expected range, nobody will know when to worry.</li>
    <li><strong>Is there an owner who can act on it?</strong> A metric that moves with nobody responsible for responding is a report, not a monitor.</li>
    <li><strong>Can it be cut by the dimensions that matter?</strong> Segment, region, channel, product. Many problems are local, and a total hides them.</li>
    <li><strong>Is it hard to game?</strong> Prefer measures of flow and outcome over measures of activity, which are easier to inflate without improving anything.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>A health metric set that passes these tests tends to be small. Five or six per domain is usually plenty, provided they are connected to each other and to the outcomes they predict. That connection is what an <a href="/resources/building-a-business-operating-model/">operating model</a> provides, and it is the difference between a health view and a long list of numbers.</p>

  <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Performance metrics measure outcomes against targets; health metrics measure whether the business is functioning normally, early enough to act.</li>
    <li>Leading and lagging are relative positions in a causal chain. Pair each leading indicator with the outcome it should predict, and check that it does.</li>
    <li>Cover each domain (revenue, customers, operations, delivery, finance) with a few early, owned, segmentable signals.</li>
    <li>Compare against expected bands that follow seasonality and segment, not fixed thresholds that fire at the wrong times.</li>
    <li>Composite scores hide failing components. If you use one, always show its components, apply a worst-component floor, and publish its weights.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Panoptes' <a href="/platform/business-health/">Business Health</a> capability is designed around these principles: objectives and indicators attached to parts of the business model, each with an expected band and an owner.</p>
